# Project Handoff Skill (Project Relay)

[中文](https://github.com/hoshinohatsuka/project-relay-Skill/edit/main/README.md) | [English](https://github.com/hoshinohatsuka/project-relay-Skill/blob/main/readme-EN) 

This skill allows multiple AIs to work on the same project at different times for various reasons—models may change, context may be lost, AI illusions may become severe, and resources may run out. With the Project Handoff Skill, the executable memories are written to the repository, rather than remaining in the chat box.

One skill, four uses

| Mode | When to use | Output |

|---|---|---|

| **A. Teardown & Learn** | Give it a GitHub project and require it to be broken down/analyzed/learned | `oss-teardown/<run-id>/`: baseline mapping, requirement → module mapping, selection matrix (evidence grading), decoupling and performance evidence, cue word translation, structured community survey, `LEARNING_REPORT.md` (including learning path) |

| **B. Handoff (take over)** | Take over a half-finished/unfinished project left by a predecessor AI or others | Project `.ai/` handover package (PROJECT_STATE / REQUIREMENTS / ARCHITECTURE / DECISIONS / TODO / TEST_PLAN / HANDOFF / ASSUMPTIONS / checkpoints), after your approval, sorted by `Correctness > Completeness > Maintainability > ... Performance Discipline and Git Checkpoints |

| **C Handover Freeze** | Quota running low, model switch needed – executed by the **currently departing AI** | `.ai/handoffs/HANDOFF-<topic>-<date>.md` Authoritative handover document: Red line pinned, status table, completed root cause details, pending batches (including identified issues), anti-re-exploration architecture facts, test baseline, appendix for unskilled takeover |

| **Resume from Breakpoint** | "Continue the unfinished breakdown" | Restore from `run-manifest.json` / checkpoint, without rerunning |

**Core Axiom**: Don't let the next model re-understand the project. A breaks down the project and learns its modules and architecture; C writes the contract, B verifies the contract; documentation is authoritative over intent and state, code is authoritative over facts; the red line remains bound even when crossing tool boundaries.

